From: offenbrg@fondue.gsfc.nasa.gov (Joel D. Offenberg)
Subject: Odd video problem
Date: 1996/03/29
Message-ID: <4jh8kk$rq8@post.gsfc.nasa.gov>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 144883984
organization: NASA Goddard Space Flight Center -- Greenbelt, Maryland USA
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.misc

Hi - 
	We have an odd video problem on our AlphaServer 2100, and DEC
support doesn't seem to be helping us much.  We're running D/UNIX v3.2C.
Has anyone run into anything similar?  I'm hoping at this point to find a 
few more people with similar tales...

There are 3 problems, and I am not sure if they are 3 independent problems
or 3 manifestations of one underlying problem:

1.  At random times, the mouse suddenly seems offset from its 'true'
position.  If I position the mouse on the screen and click, I get the effect
that I would expect if the mouse was 0.25-in up and to the left.  Moving
the mouse all the way to the left or top of the monitor also doesn't quite
work---it stops at about 0.25 in from the border (although the effect is
still as if the mouse was further up and left).  We have found no solution
other than rebooting the system, which fixes it.  We have not figured out
how to reproduce this problem.

2.  Sometimes, when the mouse error described above occurs, the screen will
suddenly go blank.  The little light on the monitor starts blinking---this
light blinks for 3 things: Either the power saver software kicked in, 
the monitor has a hardware fault or the monitor has lost signal from the 
graphics card.  Again, the only fix is a reboot and we have not been able
to reproduce this problem on demand.  Also, it does not occur every time
we see problem #1, although that may be because it gets rebooted before 
it has time to occur.

3.  This problem does not appear in conjunction with #1 or #2.  A user often
uses IDL (Interactive Data Language) on the Console.  This is a
data-processing programming language which can create a number of graphics
subwindows.  Normally, these windows are retained in "backing store," so 
that if a window is partially obscured (for instance), it is redrawn when
the window is moved to the front again.  After a long IDL session (say, a
couple of hours), the IDL windows no longer are retained in backing store,
and a partially-obscured IDL window will no longer redraw itself when moved
to the front again.

We've talked with DEC on this a few times, but we're still seeing it.  
The CPUs and graphics card have been replaced.  The OS has been reinstalled 
from scratch. 

Has anyone seen this sort of a problem?  

Thanks,
	Joel
