>PA-RISC platform; but I suspect that AIX also does it. Anyone know?

Minor correction to the minor correction:
It isn't RISC archatectures that have trubble with COW so much as
MMU's with inverted page tables.  It just so happens that the only
2 Unix running CPUs with inverted page table MMU's happen to be
(gennerally regarded as) RISC systems.  They also both happen to be
IBM CPU's as well.  The IBM ROMP (as seen in the IBM RT), and the
IBM POWER (as well as the IBM/Apple/Motorolla PowerPC).  

There may well be other inverted page table RISCs (are the HP's?
was the old Sun4c PMEG cruft in the 4/60 era of SPARCs?), but I
don't know 'em off the top of my head.  I *know* there are also
inverted page table CISC's, but I don't know which they would be.

Inverted page tables have some nice features (the table size grows
proportionally to the amount of physical memory in the machine, as
opposed to "normal" MMUs that require space proportional to the VM
size -- frequently with tricks to make the multiplyer smaller (multi
level page tables)).  Unfortunitly they have some rather irritating
side effects (of which making COW hard is only one!).

I'm only an OS & hardware dabbler, not a serious student of either of
the arts.  So I beleve I am unqualifyed to say *which* kind of MMU is
better when all the pros and cons are weighed.  Thus I will leave you
with no answer to that question.
