In <4jc6gf$3j1@bayonne.netapp.com>, guy@netapp.com (Guy Harris) writes:
>Furthermore, AIX, at least, supports shared memory and shared libraries
>and shared text on RS/6000's with inverted page tables; it *is* possible
>to have a page mapped to multiple addresses - the trick, as I remember,
>is that a 32-bit per-process virtual address has the upper few bits
>select a segment register, and some stuff in the segment register and
>the rest of the 32-bit virtual address are concatenated to make a
>>32-bit *global* virtual address.  A shared page has, I think, only one
>such global virtual address, but appears in more than one per-process
>32-bit address space.

Essentially correct, but not entirely complete...

Shared stuff on the RS is divided into 2 categories, shared *segments*
and shared *pages*. Segments are shared in the manner you describe, by
pointing at them from the segment registers of multiple processes;
that's easy. 

The kernel segment, kernel extension segment, shared library segment,
program text segments, shared memory segments, files mapped with 
shmat() (an AIX-ism), are all handled like this. That gives rise to the
(sometimes annoying) restriction (10, IIRC) on the number of attached 
shared memory segments; there are only 16 segment registers...

[Another AIX-oddity is that _all_ open files are actually handled as 
mapped memory segments, they just aren't made addressable to the user
process unless it calls shmat() on the file descriptor.]

Shared pages are more difficult; if you aren't sharing the whole
segment [eg. for fork() or mmap()], then as I understand it, the method
used is to switch the physical page between the referencing segments
as needed. So a (fast) page fault is taken on the page every time it
is referenced by a different segment. [This is documented for mmap();
mmap'ed pages are shared thus between a private segment and the real
shared segment that maps the file.]

But in the fork() case, then at some point the page must be copied,
rather than just twiddling it around; I would be interested to know
whether this is on the first reference, the first write, or some
other scheme. Perhaps a real AIX-wizard could enlighten us?

[disclaimer: the above is all either deduction or from published IBM
documentation; I have no access to the innards of AIX...]

-- Andrew  (andrewg@microlise.co.uk)

